on between the performance of discrete time filtering and continuous time filtering, together with the fundamental result of Duncan on the relation between mutual information and causal minimal mean square error estimation in a Gaussian channel. Namely, we use the mutual information in continuous time as a scan invariant feature, and the actual value of the excess loss bound results from the difference between discrete and continuous time filtering problems, as will be made precise below. From now on we assume the loss function is the squared error loss, $l_s(\cdot)$. We start with several definitions. Let $X$ be a Gaussian random variable, $X \sim \calN(0,\sx)$. Consider the following two estimation problems: - The scalar problem of estimating $X$ based on $Y=X+N$, where $N \sim \calN(0,\sn)$, independent of $X$. - The continuous time problem of causally estimating $X_t \equiv X$, $t \in [0,1]$, based on $Y_t$, which is an AWGN-corrupted version of $X_t$, the Gaussian noise having a spectral density level of $\sn$. To bound the sensitivity of the scanning and filtering performance, it is beneficial to consider the difference between the estimation errors in the above two problems, that is,  egin{equation}\label{eq. what we want to bound} \int_0^1 \Var(X_t|Y^t) \der t - \Var(X|Y), \end{equation} where $Y^t$ is the continuous time signal $\{Y_{t'}\}_{t'=0}^t$. Clearly, $\Var(X|Y) =  rac{\sx \sn}{\sx + \sn}$. Since $\int_{0}^t{Y_{t'}}\der t'$ is a sufficient statistics in the estimation of $X_t \equiv X$, $\Var(X_t|Y^t)$ is equivalent to the squared error in estimating $X$ based on $X+	ilde{N}$, $	ilde{N}$ being a Gaussian random variable, independent of $X$, with zero mean and variance $\sn/t$. Thus,  egin{eqnarray}\label{def. f} \int_0^1 \Var(X_t|Y^t) \der t - \Var(X|Y) &=& \int_0^1  rac{\sx (\sn / t)}{\sx + (\sn / t) } \der t-  rac{\sx \sn}{\sx + \sn}  
onumber\ &=& \sn \ln \left(1+ rac{\sx}{\sn}ight) -  rac{\sx \sn}{\sx + \sn} 
onumber\ &=& \sn f\left( rac{\sx}{\sn}ight), \end{eqnarray} where  egin{equation}\label{def. f scantering} f(x)=\ln(1+x)- rac{x}{x+1}. \end{equation} The following is the main result in this sub-section. theorem Let $X_{V_n}$ be a Gaussian random field with a constant marginal distribution satisfying $\Var(X_i)=\sx < \infty$ for all $i \in V_n$. Let $Y_i=X_i+N_i$, where $N_{V_n}$ is a white Gaussian noise of variance $\sigma_N^2$, independent of $X_{V_n}$. Then, for any two scans $\Psi^1$ and $\Psi^2$, we have  egin{equation}\label{eq. first bound}  rac{1}{n^2}\left|EL_{(\Psi^1,	ilde{F}^{opt})}\left(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n}ight)-EL_{(\Psi^2,	ilde{F}^{opt})}\left(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n}ight)ight|  \leq \sn f\left( rac{\sx}{\sn}ight).  \end{equation} theorem Theorem bounds the absolute difference between the scanning and filtering performance of any two scanners, $\Psi^1$ and $\Psi^2$, assuming they use their optimal filters. Clearly, since the scanners are arbitrary, this result can also be interpreted as the difference in performance between any scan $\Psi$, and the best achievable performance, $	ilde{U}(l,Q_{V_n})$. Note that the bound value, $\sn f\left( rac{\sx}{\sn}ight)$, is a single letter expression, which depends on the input field $X_{V_n}$ and the noise $N_{V_n}$ only through their variances. Namely, the bound does not depend on the memory in $X_{V_n}$. proof[Proof (Theorem )] As mentioned earlier, the comparison between any two scans is made by bounding the normalized cumulative loss of any scan $\Psi$ in terms of a scan invariant entity, which is the mutual information. For simplicity, assume first that the scan $\Psi$ is data-independent, namely, it is merely a reordering of the entries of $Y_{V_n}$. In this case, $\{X_{\Psi_i}\}_{i=1}^{n^2}$ is a discrete time Gaussian vector. We construct from it a continuous time process, $\{X_t^{(c)}\}_{t \in [0,n^2]}$, where for any $t \in [i-1,i)$, $X_{t}^{(c)}=X_{\Psi_i}$, $i \in \{1,2,\ldots,n^2\}$. That is, $X_t^{(c)}$ is a piecewise constant process, whose constant values at intervals of length $1$ correspond to the original values of the discrete time vector $\{X_{\Psi_i}\}$. Let $\{Y_{\Psi_i}\}$ and $\{Y_t^{(c)}\}$ be the AWGN-corrupted versions on $\{X_{\Psi_i}\}$ and $X_t^{(c)}$, namely, $Y_{\Psi_i}=X_{\Psi_i}+N_{\Psi_i}$ and $Y_t^{(c)}$ is constructed according to  egin{equation} \der Y_t^{(c)} = X_t^{(c)} \der t + \sigma_N \der W_t,\quad t \in [0,n^2], \end{equation} where $W_t$ is a standard Brownian motion. Observe that the white Gaussian noise, $\sigma_N \der W_t$, has a spectral density of level $\sn$, similar to the variance of the discrete time noise $N_{V_n}$. Since we switch from discrete time to continuous time, it is important to note that the noise value in the two problems is equivalent. That is, if the discrete time field $X_{V_n}$ is corrupted by noise of variance $\sn$, then we wish the continuous time white noise to have a spectrum such that the integral over an interval of length $1$, whose integrand is the continuous output $Y_t^{(c)}$ (and thus is a sufficient statistics in order to estimate the piecewise-continuous input $X_t$ in this interval), will be a random variable which is exactly $X_{\Psi_i} + N_{\Psi_i}$, $N_{\Psi_i}$ having a variance of $\sn$. We have,  egin{align}   rac{1}{n^2}E_{Q_{V_n}}&L_{(\Psi,	ilde{F}^{opt})}\left(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n}ight) 
onumber\  &=  rac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^{n^2}{\Var\left(X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i}}ight)} 
onumber\ &\stackrel{(a)}{=}  rac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^{n^2}\Bigg[\int_0^1{\Var\left(X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}},\{Y_{t'}^{(c)}\}_{{t'} \in [i-1,i-1+t]}ight)\der t}  
onumber\ & \qquad -\sn f\left( rac{\Var ig(X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}} ig)}{\sn}ight)\Bigg] 
onumber\ &\stackrel{(b)}{\ge}  rac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^{n^2}\int_0^1{\Var\left(X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}},\{Y_{t'}^{(c)}\}_{t' \in [i-1,i-1+t]}ight)\der t}   -\sn f\left( rac{\Var ig(X_1 ig)}{\sn}ight) 
onumber\ & \stackrel{(c)}{=} rac{1}{n^2}2\sn I \left(\{X_t^{(c)}\}_{t \in [0,n^2]};\{Y_t^{(c)}\}_{t \in [0,n^2]}ight)   -\sn f\left( rac{\Var ig(X_1 ig)}{\sn}ight) 
onumber\ & = rac{1}{n^2}2\sn I \left(\{X_{\Psi_i}\};\{Y_{\Psi_i}\}ight) - \sn f\left( rac{\Var ig(X_1 ig)}{\sn}ight) 
onumber\ & \stackrel{(d)}{=}  rac{1}{n^2}2\sn I \left(X_{V_n};Y_{V_n}ight) - \sn f\left( rac{\Var ig(X_1 ig)}{\sn}ight).\label{eq. proof of scantering bound G} \end{align} The equality (a) is from the application of def. f with $X=X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}}$, i.e., with $X_{\Psi_i}$ distributed conditioned on $Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}}$. Note conditioned on $Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}}$, $X_{\Psi_i}$ is indeed Gaussian, and that def. f applies to any Gaussian $X$ corrupted by Gaussian noise. The inequality (b) is since $\Var(X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}}) \leq \Var(X_1)$ and due to the increasing monotonicity of $f$, (c) is since the resulting integral from $0$ to $n^2$ is simply the minimal mean square error in filtering $\{Y_t^{(c)}\}$ (as $Y_{\Psi_i}$ is a sufficient statistics with respect to $\{Y_{t'}^{(c)}\}_{t' \in [i-1,i-1+t]}$), and the application of Duncan's result [Theorem 3]Duncan70. Finally, (d) is since the mutual information is invariant to the reordering of the random variables. To complete the proof of Theorem , simply note that since $f(x)$ is non-negative for $x>0$, by (a) above, the normalized cumulative loss can be upper bounded as well, that is,  egin{equation}  rac{1}{n^2}E_{Q_{V_n}}L_{(\Psi,	ilde{F}^{opt})}\left(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n}ight) \leq  rac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^{n^2}\int_0^1{\Var\left(X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}},\{Y_{t'}^{(c)}\}_{t' \in [i-1,i-1+t]}ight)\der t} \end{equation} hence, similarly as in the chain of inequalities leading to eq. proof of scantering bound G,  egin{equation}\label{eq. upper bound like GSV}  rac{1}{n^2}E_{Q_{V_n}}L_{(\Psi,	ilde{F}_{opt})}\left(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n}ight) \leq  rac{1}{n^2}2\sn I \left(X_{V_n};Y_{V_n}ight). \end{equation} In fact, equation eq. upper bound like GSV can be viewed as the scanning and filtering analogue of [eq. (156a)]Guo_Shamai_Verdu05. Now, if the scan $\Psi$ is data-dependent, the above derivations apply, with the use of the smoothing property of conditional expectation. That is, conditioned on $Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}}$, the position $\Psi_i$ is fixed (assuming deterministic scanners, though random scanning order can be tackled with a similar method), relation (a) in eq. proof of scantering bound G holds since it holds conditioned on $Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}}$, and relation (c) holds as the mutual information is invariant under data-dependent reordering as well. This is very similar to the methods used in the proof of [Proposition 13]Cohen_Merhav_Weissman_I06, where it was shown that the entropy of a vector is invariant to data-dependent reordering. proof At this point, a few remarks are in order. A very simple bound, applicable to arbitrarily distributed fields and under squared error loss (yet interesting mainly in the Gaussian regime) results from noting that for any random variables $X$ and $Y=X+N$,  egin{equation}\label{eq. bound for arbit. X for scantering sens.} 0 \leq \Var(X|Y) \leq \sn  rac{\sx}{\sx+\sn}. \end{equation} Namely, simple symbol by symbol restoration results in a cumulative loss of at most $\sn  rac{\sx}{\sx+\sn}$, and we have,  egin{eqnarray}  rac{1}{n}EL_{(\Psi,	ilde{F}^{opt})}\left(\{X_i\}_{i=1}^{n},\{Y_i\}_{i=1}^{n}ight) & =&  rac{1}{n}\sum_i \Var(X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i}}) 
onumber\ & \leq & rac{1}{n}\sum_i \Var(X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_{i}}) 
onumber\ &=& \sn  rac{\sx}{\sx+\sn}.\label{eq. upper bound on scantering performance with var} \end{eqnarray} Thus, the excess loss in non-optimal scanning cannot be greater than that value, hence,  egin{equation}\label{eq. first bound in cor}  rac{1}{n^2}\left|EL_{(\Psi^1,	ilde{F}^{opt})}\left(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n}ight)-EL_{(\Psi^2,	ilde{F}^{opt})}\left(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n}ight)ight|  \leq \sn  rac{\sx}{\sx+\sn}.  \end{equation} In the next sub-section, we derive a tighter bound than the bound in eq. first bound in cor, applicable to arbitrarily distributed noise-free fields. However, since this bound may be harder to evaluate, it is interesting to discuss the properties of eq. first bound in cor as well. Both the bound in Theorem and the bound in eq. first bound in cor are in the form of $\Var(X_1)g(\snr)$, for some $g$, where $\snr = \sx / \sn$. This means that any bound obtained for a certain SNR applies to all values of $\Var(X_1)$ by rescaling. The bound in Theorem has the form $\Var(X_1) rac{f(\snr)}{\snr}$, where $f(\cdot)$ was defined in def. f scantering, and we have  egin{equation} \lim_{\snr 	o 0^+}  rac{f(\snr)}{\snr}=\lim_{\snr 	o \infty}  rac{f(\snr)}{\snr}=0, \end{equation} that is, the scan is inconsequential at very high or very low SNR. This is clear as at high SNR the current observation is by far the most influential, and whatever previous observations used is inconsequential. For low SNR, the cumulative loss is high whatever the scan is. Unlike the bound in Theorem , eq. first bound in cor does not predict the correct behavior for $\snr 	o 0^+$, and is mainly interesting in the high SNR regime. The above observations are also evident in , which includes both the bound given in Theorem , applicable to Gaussian fields, and eq. first bound in cor, applicable to arbitrarily distributed fields. It is also evident that in the case of Gaussian fields, $ rac{f(\snr)}{\snr}$ has a unique maximum of approximately $0.216$, that is, the excess loss due to a suboptimal scan at any SNR is upper bounded by $0.216 \Var(X_1)$. remark It is clear from the proof of Theorem that an upper bound on the expression in eq. what we want to bound, valid for arbitrarily distributed input $X$, may yield an upper bound on the excess scanning and filtering loss which is also valid for arbitrarily distributed random fields. However, while the integral in eq. what we want to bound can be upper bounded by assuming a Gaussian $X$, $\Var(X|Y)$ has no non-trivial lower bound. In fact, in , it is shown that if $X$ is the following binary random variable,  egin{equation} X = \left\{  egin{array}{ll} \sqrt{ rac{1-p}{p}} & 	extrm{w.p. }p,\ -\sqrt{ rac{p}{1-p}} & 	extrm{w.p. }1-p,\ \end{array} ight. \end{equation} for which $EX=0$ and $EX^2=1$, then we have  egin{equation} Var(X|Y) \leq  rac{1}{2p(1-p)}e^{- rac{\sigma_X^2/\sigma_N^2}{4p(1-p)}}, \end{equation} which can be arbitrarily close to $0$ for small enough $p$. Thus, the only lower bound on $V(X|Y)$ which is valid for any $X$ with $EX^2 < \infty$, and depends only on $\sigma_X^2$ and $\sigma_N^2$, is $0$ (and hence results is a bound weaker than Theorem or eq. first bound in cor). remark In the next two subsections, we derive new bounds on the excess loss, which are valid for more general input fields. First, we generalize the bound in Theorem. While the result may be complex to evaluate in its general form, we show that for binary input fields the bound admits a simple form. We then show that if the input alphabet is continuous, then a non-trivial bound on $\Var(X|Y)$ can be derived easily, which, in turn, results in a new bound on the excess loss. Generalization of Theorem. A generalization of Theorem results from revisiting equality (a) of eq. proof of scantering bound G, which is simply the application of def. f with $X=X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}}$. While it is clear that an expression similar to that in def. f can be computed for non-Gaussian $X$, it is not clear that $X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}}$ has the same distribution for any $1 \leq i \leq n^2$ (unlike the Gaussian setting, where $X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}}$ is always Gaussian). Nevertheless, using the definition below, one can generalize Theorem for arbitrarily distributed inputs as follows. For any $(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n})$, where $Y_{V_n}$ is the AWGN-corrupted version of $X_{V_n}$, define  egin{equation}\label{def. f*} f^*(X_{V_n},\sn) = \max_{\Psi,1 \leq i \leq n^2}\left\{ \int_0^1 \Var\left(X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}},\{Y_{t'}^{(c)}\}_{t' \in [i-1,i-1+t]}ight) \der t - \Var\left(X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i}}ight) ight\}. \end{equation} theorem Let $X_{V_n}$ be an arbitrarily distributed random field, with a constant marginal distribution satisfying $\Var(X_i)=\sx < \infty$ for all $i \in V_n$. Let $Y_i=X_i+N_i$, where $N_{V_n}$ is a white Gaussian noise of variance $\sigma_N^2$, independent of $X_{V_n}$. Then, for any two scans $\Psi^1$ and $\Psi^2$, we have  egin{equation}\label{eq. generalized bound}  rac{1}{n^2}\left|EL_{(\Psi^1,	ilde{F}^{opt})}\left(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n}ight)-EL_{(\Psi^2,	ilde{F}^{opt})}\left(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n}ight)ight|  \leq f^*(X_{V_n},\sn).  \end{equation} theorem The proof of Theorem is similar to that of Theorem , and appears in Appendix. Note that $f^*(X_{V_n},\sn)$ is scan-independent, as it includes a maximization over all possible scans. At first sight, it seems like this maximization may take the sting out of the excess loss bound. However, as the example below shows, at least for the interesting scenario of binary input, this is not the case. First, however, a few more general remarks are in order. Since important insight can be gained when using the results of Guo, Shamai and Verd'u , let us mention the setting used therein. In , one wishes to estimate $X$ based on $\sqrt{\snr}X+N$, where $N$ is a standard normal random variable. Denote by $\I(\snr)$ and $\mmse(\snr)$ the mutual information between $X$ and $\sqrt{\snr}X+N$, and the minimal mean square error in estimating $X$ based on $\sqrt{\snr}X+N$, respectively. Note that $\Var(X|Y)$ in our setting equals $\sx \mmse(\sx/\sn)$. Under these definitions,  egin{equation}  rac{\der}{\der \snr}\I(\snr) =  rac{1}{2}\mmse(\snr), \end{equation} or, equivalently,  egin{equation} \I(\snr)= rac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{\snr}{\mmse(\gamma)}\der \gamma. \end{equation} Consequently, the result of Theorem can be restated as  egin{equation}\label{eq. the I-mmse form}  rac{1}{n^2}\left|EL_{(\Psi^1,	ilde{F}^{opt})}\left(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n}ight)-EL_{(\Psi^2,	ilde{F}^{opt})}\left(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n}ight)ight|  \leq 2\sn\I(\snr)-\sx\mmse(\snr),  \end{equation} where $\I(\snr)= rac{1}{2}\ln(1+\snr)$ and $\mmse(\snr)= rac{1}{1+\snr}$ are simply the mutual information and minimal mean square error of the scalar problem (hence, a single letter expression) of estimating a Gaussian $X$ based on $\sqrt{\snr}X+N$, where $N$ standard Gaussian. In fact, the bound in Theorem will always have the form $2\sn\I(\snr)-\sx\mmse(\snr)$, for some $X^*$ whose distribution is the maximizing distribution in def. f*. The next example shows that this is indeed the case for binary input as well, and the resulting bound can be easily computed. example[Binary input and AWGN] Consider the case where $X_{V_n}$ is a binary random field, with a symmetric marginal distribution (that is, $P(X_0=\sigma_X)=P(X_0=-\sigma_X)=1/2$). Note that the $X_i$'s are not necessarily i.i.d., and any dependence between them is possible. $Y_{V_n}$ is the AWGN-corrupted version of $X_{V_n}$. To evaluate the bound in Theorem , $f^*(X_{V_n},\sn)$ should be calculated. However, for any scan $\Psi$ and time $i$, $X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i-1}}$ is still a binary random variable, taking the values $\pm \sigma_X$ with probabilities $(p,1-p)$, for some $0 \leq p \leq 1/2$. Hence,  egin{equation}\label{eq. f^* for binary} f^*(X_{V_n},\sn) \leq \max_{0 \leq p \leq 1/2}\left\{\int_0^1 \Var(X_t|Y^t) \der t - \Var(X|Y)ight\}, \end{equation} where $X$ is a binary random variable, taking the values $\pm \sigma_X$ with probabilities $(p,1-p)$, $X_t \equiv X$, $Y=X+N$ and $Y_t$ is the AWGN-corrupted version of $X_t$. The following result holds for any random variable $X$. claim For any random variable $X$ with $\Var(X)=\sx < \infty$, the expression in eq. what we want to bound is monotonically increasing in $\sx$. claim proof We have,  egin{align} \int_0^1 \Var(X|Y^t) \der t - \Var(X|Y) &= \int_{0}^{1}{\sx \mmse(\sx/\sn t)\der t}-\sx \mmse(\sx/\sn) 
onumber\ &=\sn \int_{0}^{\sx/\sn}{\mmse(\gamma)\der \gamma}-\sx \mmse(\sx/\sn). \end{align} Thus,  egin{align}  rac{\der}{\der\sx}\left(\int_0^1 \Var(X_t|Y^t) \der t - \Var(X|Y)ight) &= -\sx  rac{\der}{\der\sx}\mmse(\sx/\sn) 
onumber\ &= -2 \snr  rac{\der^2}{\der\snr^2}\I(\snr) 
onumber\ & \geq 0, \end{align} where the last inequality is by [Corollary 1]Guo_Shamai_Verdu05. proof Claim simply states that the monotonicity of $f(\cdot)$ used in inequality (b) of eq. proof of scantering bound G is not specific for Gaussian input, and holds for any $X$. Thus, by Claim , the term in the braces of equation eq. f^* for binary is monotonically increasing in the variance of $X$, which is simply $4 \sx (p-p^2)$. Thus, it is maximized by $p=1/2$, and we have  egin{equation}\label{eq. sens bound binary input Gaussian noise} f^*(X_{V_n},\sn) = 2\sn\I(\snr)-\sx\mmse(\snr), \end{equation} where $\I(\snr)$ and $\mmse(\snr)$ are the mutual information and minimal mean square error in the estimation of $X$ based on $\sqrt{\snr}X+N$, where $X$ is binary symmetric and $N$ is a standard normal. Since the conditional mean estimate in this problem is $	anh(\sqrt{\snr}Y)$, we have  egin{equation} \I(\snr)=\snr -  rac{1}{\sqrt{2 \pi}}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{e^{- rac{y^2}{2}}\ln\cosh(\snr-\sqrt{\snr}y)dy}, \end{equation} and  egin{equation}\label{def. mmse binary} \mmse(\snr)=1-  rac{1}{\sqrt{2 \pi}}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}{e^{- rac{y^2}{2}}	anh(\snr-\sqrt{\snr}y)dy}, \end{equation} so the bound can be computed numerically. The above bound is plotted in. Similarly to the case of Gaussian input, it is insightful to compare this bound to a simple symbol-by-symbol filtering bound. That is, since for any binary $X$ corrupted by AWGN of variance $\sn$, $0 \leq \Var(X|Y) \leq \sx\mmse(\snr)$, we have  egin{equation}\label{eq. sens bound binary input Gaussian noise simple}  rac{1}{n^2}\left|EL_{(\Psi^1,	ilde{F}^{opt})}\left(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n}ight)-EL_{(\Psi^2,	ilde{F}^{opt})}\left(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n}ight)ight|  \leq \sx\mmse(\snr),  \end{equation} where $\mmse(\snr)$ is given in def. mmse binary. This is simply the analogue of eq. first bound in cor to the binary input setting. example Bound for Arbitrarily Distributed Continuous Input. In this sub-section, we derive an additional bound on the excess scanning and filtering loss under squared error. We assume, however, that the input random field $X_{V_n}$ is over $\Reals^{V_n}$, and that $X_i | Y_{V_n}$ has a finite differential entropy for any $i \in V_n$ (roughly speaking, this means that in the denoising problem of $X_i$, $X_i|Y_{V_n}$ is a non-degenerated continuous random variable). Under the above assumptions, we derive an excess loss bound which is not only valid for non-Gaussian input, but also depends on the memory in the random field $(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n})$. On the other hand, it is important to note that the bound below is mainly asymptotic, and may be much harder to evaluate compared to the bounds in Theorem or eq. first bound in cor. By [Theorem 9.6.5]Cov_Thom91, for any $X,Y$ with a finite conditional differential entropy $H(X|Y)$,  egin{equation}\label{eq. bound on var using entropy} \Var(X|Y) \geq  rac{1}{2 \pi e} \exp\left\{2H(X|Y)ight\}. \end{equation} Thus,  egin{eqnarray}  rac{1}{n}EL_{(\Psi,	ilde{F}^{opt})}\left(X_{V_n},Y_{V_n}ight) & = &  rac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^{n^2} \Var(X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i}}) 
onumber\ & \stackrel{(a)}{\ge} &  rac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^{n^2} rac{1}{2\pi e}\exp\{2H(X_{\Psi_i}|Y_{\Psi_1}^{\Psi_{i}})\} 
